  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough ejecting northeastward across the Central \nPlains to Upper Midwest will result in a weakening of the Omega \nBlock and a slight eastward displacement of the upper-level ridge \naround Tuesday into Wednesday. The glancing passage of the trough \nwill at least provide increasing clouds, but it is unlikely to be \nsufficient for any precipitation with limited moisture return. \nHowever, the pattern will subsequently become more favorable for \nmoisture return, which, when combined with a wavering front reaching \nas far southeastward as northern/northeastern MO during the second \nhalf of the week, leads to increasing ensemble membership with \nmeasurable rainfall in that portion of the CWA. This rainfall would \nbe in the form of a few showers at times, with the vast majority of \nthe period still expected to be dry while low-level southwesterly \nflow south of the front maintains seasonably warm conditions. \nThursday is forecast to be the warmest day of the forecast period as \nthe ridge briefly amplifies again, accompanied by high temperatures \nin the 80s F for parts of the CWA.\n\nAs the ridge weakens again next weekend with another upper-level \ntrough passage and more time for moisture to increase, there is a \nstronger signal for rainfall across the CWA. Around 70 to 90 percent \nof global ensemble model membership has measurable rainfall at some \npoint next weekend with 40 to 50 percent probabilities of greater \nthan 0.25\". However, PoPs in the current forecast are slightly lower \ndue to them being 6 hourly with timing uncertainty. There is little \nsupport for this trough to have a front that provides much, if any, \nairmass change, but clouds and precipitation have the potential to \ntemporarily cool temperatures. For context, although the NBM \ninterquartile temperature range does increase to around 8 F next \nweekend, even the 25th percentile is still above average for the end \nof October/beginning of November.",
